  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "Suppose that \\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\) of \\(10\\) bananas are worth as much as \\(8\\) oranges. How many oranges are worth as much as \\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\) of \\(5\\) bananas?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Equivalent fruit quantities scale together: multiplying the number of bananas by a factor multiplies the equivalent number of oranges by the same factor. The given banana quantity is\n\\[\n\\frac23\\cdot10=\\frac{20}{3},\n\\]\nand the requested quantity is \\(\\frac12\\cdot5=\\frac52\\) bananas. The requested quantity is the following fraction of the given one:\n\\[\n\\frac52\\div\\frac{20}{3}\n=\\frac52\\cdot\\frac3{20}\n=\\frac38.\n\\]\nThus its equivalent orange quantity is \\(8\\cdot\\frac38=3\\). The answer is \\(3\\) oranges, choice \\(C\\).",
